          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-lucie-1843e92d8f2ba6e5\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.91,\"analysis\":\"Henny directly reciprocates Knut’s declaration of love, strongly avowing her loving state (T). In this proposal scene, the avowal also participates in her acceptance of a mutual marital commitment, especially as it is immediately joined to “I am yours” (P). Her agitation and difficulty speaking show intense emotion, but not independently that the words are a reflex-like discharge rather than an emotionally delivered avowal and undertaking (E=0).\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty concerns P: the explicit undertaking is clearest in the adjoining “I am yours” and the proposal context, though “I love you too” forms part of the same acceptance. The bodily agitation alone does not establish E.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Henny directly tells Knut, “And I love you too,” during her response to his declaration and marriage proposal.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Knut has affirmed that he would marry Henny despite the supposed lovers in her past and asks whether she now believes in his love. Overcome with emotion, she embraces him, affirms belief in his love, reciprocates it, and says she is his. The utterance therefore combines a sincere-seeming avowal with acceptance of their relationship and prospective marriage. Her later revelation that the lovers were invented as a test complicates the courtship context but does not undermine the ordinary love-related force of this utterance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ja, Knut, nu tror jeg på din kjærlighed. Og jeg elsker også Dig.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit reciprocal declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal of love.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg tror på Dig og jeg er din. Siden Du vil ha mig.”\",\"supports\":\"The adjoining language of being his, in response to his willingness to marry her, supports performative participation in a relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Knut says he would beg her to take him as her husband and asks whether she believes in his love; Henny then gives the highlighted response.\",\"supports\":\"The highlighted utterance is reciprocal and occurs as part of accepting a marriage proposal, supporting both T and P.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Henny is violently agitated, gasps, struggles unsuccessfully to produce words, and first embraces Knut before speaking.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes intense emotional delivery but does not independently show reflex-like verbal discharge, so it does not warrant E above zero.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Henny subsequently calls herself “en forlovet pige” (“an engaged girl”).\",\"supports\":\"Her own interpretation of the exchange as an engagement reinforces the undertaking dimension.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a present-tense explicit first-person love expression in Amalie Skram’s Lucie and retained as VALID_EXPLICIT_LOVE_I_YOU.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms identification of the classified core occurrence; it does not independently determine its pragmatic force.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally represent the core force: a reciprocal avowal embedded in acceptance of a marital relationship. Emotional intensity is adequately treated as contextual delivery evidence and does not create residual pressure for E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
